75: \begin{document}
76: 
77: \maketitle
78: 
79: \begin{abstract}
80: Retraining a model can change its uncertainty estimates even when no new data
81: are added. In a contextual bandit, we ask when recomputing past gradients at
82: the current model preserves the confidence guarantee used to choose actions.
83: We give a conditional upper confidence bound (UCB) theorem that starts from
84: features fixed before each reward and accounts for changing the uncertainty
85: matrix, approximating it, and computing its widths. For squared loss, a
86: correction based on those stored features supplies a confidence center without
87: assuming that the nonlinear optimizer converges. This requires predictable
88: parameter paths in a certified region and explicit Taylor and misspecification
89: error bounds. The conversion has a basic limit: with the same history, center,
90: radius and bias, its certified width cannot be smaller than the direct
91: reference width. Exact actionwise transport recovers that width; the result
92: leaves the regret ordering of policies with different histories open.
93: A prespecified study tests four policies in three conditions using a
94: 29-parameter scaled-tanh model and 50 paired seeds per condition.
95: Hessian transport, which bounds matrix changes using prediction Hessians, has
96: higher mean pseudo-regret than every comparator in each condition; all nine
97: pointwise paired confidence intervals for regret differences exclude zero. Transport inflation dominates its regret bounds, which give no
98: improvement over a deterministic regret cap. The reference policy's own bounds
99: are also vacuous. All policies, including an uncertified control, pass the
100: recorded floating-point confidence and optimism checks. The study illustrates
101: why a conditionally valid confidence score, a tight regret bound and a useful
102: decision rule must be assessed separately.
103: \end{abstract}
